  ('p', 'Every day after school, Ren walked past the old clock shop on Hill Street. Its window was full of clocks, and none of them showed the same time. Ren thought it was strange, even a little silly. Why would a clock shop not fix its own clocks?'),
  ('p', 'One rainy afternoon in October, Ren\'s grandfather\'s watch stopped. It was a silver watch with a crack in the glass, and Grandpa had worn it every day for fifty years. Since Grandpa had gone into hospital the week before, Ren had been keeping it in his bag. When he noticed it had stopped, he felt as if something bad had happened. Without thinking, he opened the door of the clock shop.'),
  ('p', 'Inside, an old woman with round glasses was working at a desk. "It\'s my grandfather\'s," Ren said, putting the watch down. She opened the back carefully and looked at it for a long time. "This needs a new spring," she said. "I can order one, but it will take three weeks." Ren\'s heart sank. Three weeks felt too long.'),
  ('p', 'Seeing his face, the woman pointed at the window. "Do you know why those clocks all show different times?" Ren shook his head. "Each one shows the time in a city where one of my customers lives now. That one is Toronto, where a girl who used to come here every Saturday moved. That one is Nairobi." She smiled. "When I look at them, I remember that people are living their own lives in their own time, even when they are far away."'),
